D.append(dict(description='Historian of Enlightenment France whose studies of clothing, possessions and everyday practices connected material culture to social differentiation. In dialogue with Gil Bartholeyns, Roche insists that representations require attention to their production, circulation and unequal reception. His consumption history examines changing needs and access to goods rather than treating objects as isolated curiosities.',sources=[S('Gil Bartholeyns, Avis de recherche: Dialogue avec Daniel Roche, Modes pratiques, pp170–187','https://devisu.inha.fr/modespratiques/354','Own answers in Histoire sociale et histoire des représentations and Mode et consommation sections read. Roche criticizes removal of social distinctions from representations and discusses clothing consumption/diffusion.'),S('Collège de France: Daniel Roche principales publications','https://www.college-de-france.fr/sites/default/files/media/document/2022-11/Roche%20%E2%80%93%20Principales%20publications.pdf','Original Le Peuple de Paris1981, Culture des apparences1989,Histoire choses banales1997; reissues distinguished.',True),S('IHMC: Faire de l’histoire avec Daniel Roche,9 June2023','https://ihmc.ens.psl.eu/faire-histoire-avec-daniel-roche-videos','Institution explicitly records death19 February2023; video presentations not independently watched.')],works=[W(1981,'Le Peuple de Paris',1),W(1989,'La Culture des apparences',1),W(1997,'Histoire des choses banales',1)],edges=[E('consumption','Roche relates clothing and household objects to unequal access, changing needs and the diffusion of consumption.','Own interview Mode et consommation section; stated spending contrasts are Roche’s examples, not independently recomputed statistics.',0),E('social','Roche insists that cultural representations be reconstructed through social differentiation and their contexts of production.','Own interview opening; his criticism of representation-only history is explicit, rather than an undifferentiated cultural-turn affiliation.',0)],life=L('deceased',2,'Institutional tribute opening','Explicit19 February2023.',2023,'2023-02-19')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of gender, social policy and unequal power in American families. Gordon’s autobiographical account links her turn to women’s history with the women’s liberation movement. Her research on welfare and family violence treats domestic relations as public and political, while The Great Arizona Orphan Abduction reconstructs the interaction of race, family claims and local power through a tightly focused narrative.',sources=[S('History News Network, History Doyen: Linda Gordon,12 November2006','https://www.historynewsnetwork.org/article/history-doyen-linda-gordon','Own Personal Anecdote read on social movement and women’s history; bibliography dates1976,1988,1994,1999. Reproduced Paula S.Fass and Stephen Lassonde substantial review passages separately attributed.'),S('Wisconsin History: Linda Gordon','https://history.wisc.edu/people/gordon-linda/','Present-tense emerita biography, books through2017 and scholarly activities; positive institutional life evidence with undated-profile limitation.'),S('Gilder Lehrman History U: Women and Politics in20th Century course outline','https://www.gilderlehrman.org/sites/default/files/2023-02/History%20U_%20Women%20%26%20Politics%20Course%20Outline.pdf','Author-led course profile identifies current NYU emerita historian; scope is course/identity, not full course watched.')],works=[W(1988,'Heroes of Their Own Lives',0),W(1994,'Pitied But Not Entitled',0),W(1999,'The Great Arizona Orphan Abduction',0)],edges=[E('women','Gordon’s account locates the research of women and gender in dialogue with feminist organizing and questions about social power.','Own2006 Personal Anecdote; this identifies her route into women’s history without making the movement the sole origin of the field.',0),E('racial_formation','The Great Arizona Orphan Abduction reconstructs how racial classifications, family claims and class shaped a local custody conflict.','Paula S.Fass and Stephen Lassonde’s substantial review passages reproduced in HNN profile; mediated assessment, not full monograph reading.',0)],life=L('living',1,'Current emerita faculty account','Positive institutional biography; explicitly dated personal activity not established in this profile.')))
D.append(dict(description='Intellectual and international historian who studies political concepts through their changing imperial and global settings. Armitage’s introduction to The Ideological Origins of the British Empire reconnects state formation in Britain and Ireland with empire and challenges a rigid domestic/overseas separation. His Atlantic research traces how a British imperial community was articulated, rather than assuming that it existed unchanged.',sources=[S('David Armitage, The Ideological Origins of the British Empire(2000), introduction publisher sample','https://assets.cambridge.org/97805215/90815/sample/9780521590815ws.pdf','Own introduction pp1–5 and first-publication2000 copyright inspected; later chapters not read.'),S('David Armitage, From the Empire of Great Britain to the British Empire, Harvard Atlantic working paper97020','https://atlantic.fas.harvard.edu/Armitage%20-%20WP%2097020','Author abstract on imperial community emerging as articulated common interests and history in1730s; abstract-only verification, not full paper.'),S('David Armitage author bibliography','https://armitage.scholars.harvard.edu/','Dates Ideological Origins2000,Declaration Independence2007,Foundations Modern International Thought2013; coauthored Manifesto explicitly marked.',True),S('Harvard: David Armitage current faculty biography','https://history.fas.harvard.edu/people/david-armitage','Explicit On Leave Academic Year2026–2027; positive current-life evidence, older book prose not used to date Civil Wars.')],works=[W(2000,'The Ideological Origins of the British Empire',0),W(2007,'The Declaration of Independence: A Global History',2),W(2013,'Foundations of Modern International Thought',2)],edges=[E('intellectualhistory','Armitage reconstructs imperial ideology through political vocabularies that joined state formation and overseas projects.','Own introduction pp1–5 challenges domestic/imperial split; early British Atlantic scope distinguished from later Indian empire.',0),E('atlantic','Armitage traces the articulation of a British Atlantic community through shared interests, histories and imperial claims.','Own working-paper abstract on1730s; supports a bounded argument, not full-paper verification or a timeless Atlantic unit.',1)],life=L('living',3,'2026–27 leave notice','Current academic-year leave and professor role.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of eighteenth-century Britain whose work crosses state finance, cultural production and consumption. In his2026 memoir Brewer explains that The Sinews of Power challenged the idea of a weak British state while investigating resistance and special interests within fiscal expansion. He resists fixed disciplinary labels and treats literature and the arts as connected historical practices rather than autonomous masterpieces.',sources=[S('John Brewer, Harvard1980–87, memoir chapter7,30 April2026','https://johnbrewer.substack.com/p/harvard-1980-87','Own Sinews research-design section on state growth, taxation and pushback inspected; retrospective2026 recollection, not original1989 text.'),S('John Brewer, Introduction, memoir draft3 September2026','https://johnbrewer.substack.com/p/introduction','Own discussion of interdisciplinary practice, Sinews/Pleasures dual reception and historical imagination read; positive2026 author-life evidence.'),S('Routledge: The Sinews of Power','https://www.routledge.com/The-Sinews-of-Power-War-Money-and-the-English-State-1688-1783-1st-Edition/Brewer/p/book/9780415754927','Explicit first published1989.',True),S('Wellcome Collection: Consumption and the World of Goods','https://wellcomecollection.org/works/n2ar4p83','Original1993 edited volume; John Brewer and Roy Porter coeditors, individual chapter credits retained.',True),S('Harvard Early Modern World: John Brewer','https://earlymodernworld.fas.harvard.edu/John-Brewer','Bibliographic paragraph dates Pleasures of the Imagination1997.',True),S('Peter Holland, Culture Ho!, NYRB9 April1998','https://www.nybooks.com/articles/1998/04/09/culture-ho/','Named substantive reader’s opening on Bewick, arts, provincial audiences and moral commentary read; not whole review/book.')],works=[W(1989,'The Sinews of Power',2),W(1993,'Consumption and the World of Goods (coeditor Roy Porter)',3),W(1997,'The Pleasures of the Imagination',4)],edges=[E('political','The Sinews of Power makes taxation, administration and the financing of war central to the eighteenth-century British state.','Brewer’s own2026 research retrospective also emphasizes resistance and policy-shaping by interests; strong state does not mean unlimited state.',0),E('culture','The Pleasures of the Imagination examines cultural making and reception across arts and provincial settings.','Peter Holland’s1998 substantial opening on Bewick, John Marsh and cultural interpretation; mediated reading, not complete-book verification.',5)],life=L('living',1,'Author/date header and first-person memoir','Own memoir published3 September2026, identity matched through Sinews/Pleasures and Caltech career.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of early North America whose continental approach unsettles self-contained national narratives. Taylor’s interview on American Civil Wars connects conflicts in the United States,Mexico and Canada through intervention,union and political economy. His work also treats British and American sovereignty as incomplete projects rather than inevitable outcomes of a single national revolution.',sources=[S('Alan Taylor interviewed by Civil War Monitor,2 October2024','https://www.civilwarmonitor.com/article/alan-taylor-american-civil-wars/','Own continental framing, Mexican/Canadian comparisons, research-process and historical-memory answers read. American Colonies2001,Revolutions2016,Republics2021 dated by author.'),S('Virginia History: Alan Taylor bibliography','https://history.virginia.edu/people/alan-taylor','Dates American Colonies2001,Divided Ground2006,Civil War1812 2010. Current title updated to emeritus; some biography prose remains old.',True),S('Maine Historical Society Alan Taylor May2026 event release','https://www.mainehistory.org/documents/191/2026_Alan_Taylor_Press_Release.pdf','Scheduled20 May2026 public talk identifies historian, distinct from economist Alan M.Taylor; positive recent activity evidence.')],works=[W(2001,'American Colonies',1),W(2006,'The Divided Ground',1),W(2010,'The Civil War of1812',1)],edges=[E('global','Taylor’s continental frame links national conflicts through cross-border intervention and political interdependence.','Own2024 interview on Mexican civil wars, Canadian confederation and American secession; continental linkage, not an exhaustive world history.',0),E('political','Taylor compares rival projects of union, liberal rights and sovereignty across North American polities.','Own2024 answers on Lincoln/Juárez and threats to union; describes a substantive political-history contribution without treating liberal ideals as universal outcomes.',0)],life=L('living',2,'Event details and scholar biography','Positive May2026 institutional event listing; performance not personally observed.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of modern Europe and historiography whose writing reconsiders the relation between social and cultural history. Jaume Aurell reads A Crooked Line as a deliberate use of intellectual autobiography to interpret the cultural turn and its political conditions. This plural approach does not require historians to abandon social questions when studying meaning, nor does Eley’s path stand for every national historiography.',scope='Jaume Aurell’s named substantive reading pp254–256 inspected; his analytical vocabulary is attributed rather than presented as direct whole-book reading. Michigan bibliography dates selected works.',sources=[S('Jaume Aurell, Making History by Contextualizing Oneself: Autobiography as Historiographical Intervention, History and Theory54(2015), pp244–268','https://culturahistorica.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/aurell-autobiography.pdf','Eley section pp254–256 read, including autobiographical intervention, cultural/social relation and methodological limits. Complete article not read.'),S('Michigan: Geoff Eley current emeritus profile','https://lsa.umich.edu/history/people/emeritus/ghe.html','Current emeritus role/contact; dates Forging Democracy2002,Crooked Line2005,Nazism as Fascism2013.',True)],works=[W(2002,'Forging Democracy',1),W(2005,'A Crooked Line',1),W(2013,'Nazism as Fascism',1)],edges=[E('social','Eley reconsiders social history after the cultural turn, retaining social questions while criticizing reductionist explanations.','Jaume Aurell’s2015 pp254–256 reading of A Crooked Line; his interpretation is named and not upgraded to full-book verification.',0),E('biography','A Crooked Line uses intellectual autobiography as a historiographical intervention, situating methods in political and personal contexts.','Aurell2015 Eley section specifically analyzes this deliberate use of self-contextualization; not a conventional biography or universal trajectory.',0)],life=L('living',1,'Current emeritus faculty role and contact','Positive current Michigan profile; no directly dated2026 personal appearance verified.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of modern Europe and international order who questions celebratory accounts of liberal progress. Glenda Sluga’s reading of No Enchanted Palace identifies the imperial and racial assumptions carried into twentieth-century international institutions. Mazower’s later interview links global institutions to the power and interests of states, preserving the tension between universal claims and unequal political authority.',sources=[S('Glenda Sluga, review of No Enchanted Palace, English Historical Review125(2010), pp1280–1282','https://doi.org/10.1093/ehr/ceq253','Public substantive extract on Smuts,Zimmern,British imperial ideology and racial hierarchy read; underlying book not read.'),S('David Blackburn interviews Mark Mazower, Spectator12 October2012','https://spectator.com/article/governing-the-world-an-interview-with-mark-mazower/','Interview report paragraphs on national/international relation and institutions serving dominant powers read; distinguish interviewer framing from quoted replies.'),S('Columbia Global Thought: Mark A.Mazower bibliography','https://globalthought.columbia.edu/directory/mark-mazower','Dates Dark Continent1998,Hitler’s Empire2008 first edition,No Enchanted Palace2009.',True),S('Columbia History Spring2026 seminar schedule','https://history.columbia.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/29/2025/11/GU-Seminars-S26_111125.pdf','Mazower listed as Spring2026 instructor HIST4387; positive recent institutional-life evidence.')],works=[W(1998,'Dark Continent',2),W(2008,'Hitler’s Empire',2,'First2008 publication distinguished from2009 Penguin edition in same bibliography.'),W(2009,'No Enchanted Palace',2)],edges=[E('global','Mazower reconstructs international institutions through their imperial inheritances and unequal distributions of power.','Glenda Sluga2010 substantial public extract; specifically No Enchanted Palace, not an unqualified account of every international institution.',0),E('political','Mazower’s history of international organization examines how national interests and dominant powers shaped institutional possibilities.','David Blackburn’s2012 interview report, paragraphs on states and international bodies; source mediation explicit.',1)],life=L('living',3,'Spring2026 instructor list','Positive current-year Columbia course assignment.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of labour,immigration and state classification whose socio-history follows relationships among identifiable people. In interviews Noiriel presents socio-history as a collective venture rather than a personal school,and treats immigration categories as historically constructed through public language and administrative power. His method connects sociology to archival history while distinguishing explanation from political judgment.',sources=[S('Pierre-Henri Ortiz interviews Gérard Noiriel, Le jeu de la sociohistoire(2/3), Nonfiction26 November2012','https://www.nonfiction.fr/article-6225-le_jeu_de_la_sociohistoire___entretien_avec_g_noiriel__23.htm','Indexed own opening answer on collective socio-history,Bourdieu and Longwy labour/immigration read; direct endpoint timed out. Limited passage scope.'),S('Carole Condat and Alexis Chabot interview Gérard Noiriel,SNES15 June2007,p50','https://www.snes.edu/IMG/pdf/Entretien_G_Noiriel_US_juin_2007.pdf','Own opening on labour/immigration and public language read; bibliography dates Workers1986,Creuset1988,Introduction socio-histoire2006.'),S('Gérard Noiriel and Stéphane Beaud, Refusing to divide French society into the racialized and the non-racialized,Le Monde2 April2026','https://www.lemonde.fr/en/opinion/article/2026/04/02/refusing-to-divide-french-society-into-the-racialized-and-the-non-racialized-does-not-mean-denying-the-existence-of-racism_6752060_23.html','Dated coauthored intervention supplies positive current author-life evidence; not used to infer demographic identities or to settle the debate.')],works=[W(1986,'Les Ouvriers dans la société française',1),W(1988,'Le Creuset français',1),W(2006,'Introduction à la socio-histoire',1)],edges=[E('historicalsociology','Noiriel’s socio-history connects archival reconstruction to relationships of power and social interdependence.','Own2012 opening describes a collective project influenced by sociology,not a claim of sole foundation or institutional membership.',0),E('racial_formation','Noiriel examines how public language and state classification construct immigration as a social and political problem.','Own2007 interview opening explicitly treats immigration as construction and representation within labour/social history; not denial of actual migration.',1)],life=L('living',2,'Author/date header','Positive dated coauthored public intervention2 April2026.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of Black labour,political culture and radical imagination. In his2022 retrospective Kelley explains Freedom Dreams as a history of movements’ visions rather than a programme predicting future victories. He places collective imagining within struggles on the ground,showing why cultural practices and aspirations matter alongside organizations and campaigns. His accounts do not assign every movement a common strategy.',sources=[S('Robin D.G.Kelley,Twenty Years of Freedom Dreams,Boston Review1 August2022','https://www.bostonreview.net/articles/twenty-years-of-freedom-dreams/','Own opening paragraphs on the2002 book,intentions,collective radical imagination and campaigns read; not the complete anniversary edition.'),S('UCLA: Robin D.G.Kelley biography and bibliography','https://history.ucla.edu/person/robin-d-g-kelley/','Current distinguished professor,active2026 project; original Hammer and Hoe1990,Race Rebels1994,Freedom Dreams2002 distinct from2015/2022 editions.',True)],works=[W(1990,'Hammer and Hoe',1),W(1994,'Race Rebels',1),W(2002,'Freedom Dreams',1)],edges=[E('black','Kelley’s movement histories center visions of freedom that exceed campaign victories or seizure of state power.','Own2022 retrospective explicitly says Freedom Dreams was neither roadmap nor prediction; particular movements rather than a universal programme.',0),E('culture','Kelley treats collective imagination as a practice generated within political struggle and cultural life.','Own2022 opening distinguishes sustained visions from narrow measures of campaign success; cultural imagination is not detached from material struggle.',0)],life=L('living',1,'Current faculty role and2026 research project','Positive current UCLA account,including ongoing2026 project.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of modern Europe whose Postwar joins Eastern and Western experiences through war’s consequences,political reconstruction and contested memory. In his interview with Donald Yerxa,Judt argues that selective forgetting enabled postwar national narratives and that European integration emerged through national interests. These are his interpretations,with differences across societies retained rather than a single story of inevitable liberal success.',sources=[S('Donald A.Yerxa interviews Tony Judt,14 November2005,published Historically Speaking January/February2006','https://www.bu.edu/historic/hs/judt.html','Own opening answers on selective forgetting,East/West comparison and national interests in integration read; interview and publication dates separated.'),S('NYU Archives: Tony Judt Papers','https://findingaids.library.nyu.edu/archives/mc_233/','Bibliography dates Past Imperfect1992,Burden Responsibility1998; explicit death6 August2010.',True)],works=[W(1992,'Past Imperfect',1),W(1998,'The Burden of Responsibility',1),W(2005,'Postwar',0,'Book’s2005 publication specified in interview introduction; interview published2006.')],edges=[E('memory','Judt examines selective forgetting and later confrontation with collaboration and persecution in postwar European narratives.','Own2005 interview opening; national differences and different communist/postwar experiences are explicit.',0),E('political','Judt reconstructs European recovery and integration through political choices and national interests across East and West.','Own2005 interview answers on planning and integration; neither inevitable unity nor a single common experience asserted.',0)],life=L('deceased',1,'Biographical note final sentence','Explicit6 August2010.',2010,'2010-08-06')))
